{"id": "86254", "name": "שני ישרים במשוואה אחת – פירוק למשוואות הישרים (באנגלית)", "defaultgrade": 1, "penalty": 0, "options": {"multiplicationsign": "dot", "complexno": "i", "matrixparens": "[", "inversetrig": "cos-1", "logicsymbol": "lang", "sqrtsign": true}, "nhints": 0, "questiontext": "<div dir=\"ltr\" style=\"max-width:980px;font-family:Arial;line-height:1.7\"><div style=\"background:#F4F1F5;border:1px solid #D9D1DD;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px\"><p>The following equation represents two straight lines. Determine the equation of each of the two lines:</p><p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align:center\">\\[ {{CT:0}} . \\]</p></div><div style=\"border:1px solid #D9D1DD;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;margin:14px 0;overflow-x:auto\"><p>Enter your answer as a set:</p><div style=\"background:#FAF9FB;border-radius:8px;padding:10px;direction:ltr;text-align:center;unicode-bidi:isolate\">\\(\\{a x + b y + c = 0,\\, d x + e y + f = 0 \\}\\) = [[input:ans1]]</div><div dir=\"ltr\" style=\"direction:ltr;text-align:center;unicode-bidi:isolate;margin-top:6px\">[[validation:ans1]]</div></div></div>", "generalfeedback": "<div dir=\"ltr\" style=\"max-width:980px;font-family:Arial;line-height:1.7\"><div style=\"background:#F4F1F5;border:1px solid #D9D1DD;border-radius:12px;padding:12px 16px;margin-bottom:12px\"><b style=\"color:#4b1f6f\">Outline:</b> The second-degree terms form a perfect square, so the equation is a quadratic equation in one linear expression \\(u\\). Its two roots give the two lines.</div><div style=\"border:1px solid #D9D1DD;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;margin:12px 0;overflow-x:auto\"><b style=\"color:#4b1f6f\">Step 1 — A quadratic in one expression</b><br>The terms of degree two form a perfect square: <span dir=\"ltr\">\\({{CT:1}}={{CT:2}}\\)</span>. The terms of degree one are a multiple of the same expression, so with<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align:center\">\\[ u={{CT:3}} \\]</p>the equation \\({{CT:0}}\\) becomes<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align:center\">\\[ {{CT:4}}=0 \\]</p></div><div style=\"border:1px solid #D9D1DD;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;margin:12px 0;overflow-x:auto\"><b style=\"color:#4b1f6f\">Step 2 — Factorising</b><br>We need two numbers whose sum is \\({{CT:5}}\\) and whose product is \\({{CT:6}}\\): they are \\({{CT:7}}\\) and \\({{CT:8}}\\). Hence<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align:center\">\\[ {{CT:9}}=0 \\]</p>so \\(u={{CT:10}}\\) or \\(u={{CT:11}}\\).</div><div style=\"border:1px solid #D9D1DD;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;margin:12px 0;overflow-x:auto\"><b style=\"color:#4b1f6f\">Step 3 — The two lines</b><br>Substituting back \\(u={{CT:3}}\\), the quadratic expression factorises as<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align:center\">\\[ ({{CT:12}})({{CT:13}}) = 0, \\]</p>and a product is zero exactly when one of the factors is zero. So the solution is<p dir=\"ltr\" style=\"text-align:center\">\\[ {{CT:14}} \\]</p>(two parallel lines).</div></div>", "specificfeedback": "<p>[[feedback:Result]]</p>", "prtcorrect": "<p><span class=\"correct\">Correct answer, well done.</span></p>", "prtpartiallycorrect": "<p><span class=\"partially\">Your answer is partially correct.</span></p>", "prtincorrect": "<p><span class=\"incorrect\">Incorrect answer.</span></p>", "inputs": [{"name": "ans1", "type": "algebraic", "boxsize": 40, "insertstars": 0, "forbidfloat": true, "forbidwords": "factor", "allowwords": "", "mustverify": true, "showvalidation": 1, "syntaxhint": "", "syntaxattribute": 0, "options": "", "requirelowestterms": true}], "prts": [{"name": "Result", "value": 1, "autosimplify": true, "feedbackstyle": 1, "feedbackvariables": "", "firstnode": 0, "nodes": [{"name": 0, "answertest": "AlgEquiv", "sans": "ans1", "tans": "correctanswer", "testoptions": null, "quiet": false, "on_true": {"scoremode": "=", "score": 1, "nextnode": -1, "answernote": "Result-0-T ", "feedback": ""}, "on_false": {"scoremode": "=", "score": 0, "nextnode": -1, "answernote": "Result-0-F ", "feedback": ""}}]}], "qvnames": ["correctanswer", "eq1", "eq2", "n1", "n2", "n4", "p", "wronganswer"], "nvariants": 50}